WEBVTT

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:08.320
Speaker 1: This theology is false already because it speaks to the original line that got us all in trouble in the first place.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:18.040
Speaker 1: Promising an alternative reality ignores the reality of sin and it cannot account for the human limitations that we have.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.520
Speaker 1: If you are a God, why can't you fly?

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:20.800
Speaker 1: Why do you die?

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:21.760
Speaker 1: You know?

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:30.400
Speaker 2: And it reminds me of conversations around zodiac signs where people say, oh, I did this because I am Leo, I am Libra, I'm Cancer, I'm Capricorn.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:36.620
Speaker 2: So you can't really challenge the wrong behavior because it's already justified by an ideology.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:39.820
Speaker 1: Anyways.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.060
Speaker 1: So what's the next one then, Pastor Ayoke?

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:50.300
Speaker 2: The next one is very crucial and is the idea that all paths lead to God.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:57.830
Speaker 2: Now, if you as a person who preaches, evangelizes, this is something you come across a lot.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:08.550
Speaker 2: And the reason is because many people do not want to go through the emotional rigor it takes to admit that actually if everybody is saying the same thing.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:19.590
Speaker 2: So if Christianity, Islam, Buddhism, Hinduism and all of these other practices all lead to the same God, then it makes no sense.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:34.670
Speaker 2: And the first thing we must notice is number one, it's a logical contradiction because in the claims of these religions is a form of mutual exclusivity.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:51.360
Speaker 2: So for instance, in Christianity we preach the Bible says in Acts chapter 4, verse 12, that neither is there salvation in any other, because there is no other name given to us by which men will be saved.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.560
Speaker 2: That's an exclusive statement.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:04.000
Speaker 2: Also in Islam there is a popular rhetoric there is no other God except Allah and Muhammad is his messenger.

00:02:04.399 --> 00:02:08.000
Speaker 2: Now that is not a claim you are going to find in any other religion.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.759
Speaker 2: Yes, it's an exclusive statement.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:18.600
Speaker 2: And I think the next statement is really very important because what this actually does is that it dismisses what the religions actually teach.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:31.240
Speaker 2: And which is what I was pointing to initially, that when people do this, they don't actually want to go through the intellectual and emotional rigor to really weigh what exactly they teach.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:32.520
Speaker 2: You will find out.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:37.880
Speaker 2: People will say, make statements like the view religion is love.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.360
Speaker 2: Now the question is, what is love?

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:41.100
Speaker 2: What is love?

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:41.740
Speaker 1: What is love?

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:50.140
Speaker 2: Because if you ask a Christian what love is and if you ask a Muslim what love is, you are going to get different definitions, very different.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:58.540
Speaker 2: For a Muslim, love is giving you giving and giving, even though it can be for a selfish interest.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:03.580
Speaker 2: But the Bible lets you know that if you like give your body to be burnt, it is still not love.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:08.570
Speaker 2: In some religions it's fine to give even if you don't have the best interest of the person.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:18.610
Speaker 2: And so that just shows that when you make the claim that all part leads to God, what you are invariably doing is that you are speaking down on these religions.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:19.930
Speaker 2: You are calling them liars.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:24.330
Speaker 2: You are saying that all they are doing is just self deceit.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:30.490
Speaker 2: Because Hinduism, for example, lets you know that there are multiple gods.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:37.860
Speaker 2: So if you say we are all saying the same thing, but Christianity says there is just one God and Hinduism says there.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:42.020
Speaker 1: Are multiple gods, we can't have two different maps.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:42.940
Speaker 2: Exactly.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:44.740
Speaker 1: And say they all lead to the same destination.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:47.700
Speaker 2: So profound.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:54.740
Speaker 2: Because even the description of the eventual destination of what we refer to heaven as Christians is different across.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:55.940
Speaker 2: It's different across both.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:00.180
Speaker 2: So it's just different maps leading to different destinations.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:00.840
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:02.200
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:02.880
Speaker 1: Now that we've.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:18.519
Speaker 1: We've gone far in describing this and there needs to be a clear delineation because we've spoken about it from a theological standpoint, but also in practice, if we're not careful, especially as charismatic Christians, there are things we do.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:19.320
Speaker 2: Yes.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:21.079
Speaker 1: That we may not know.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:32.840
Speaker 1: Their borrowed practices from the New age movement, or maybe not even bold practices, if done without knowledge, you might be pandering to the practices of this.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:41.160
Speaker 2: All the religions actually borrowed from Christianity and they practice in a way that is a perversion.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:42.600
Speaker 1: I think I like that even more.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.240
Speaker 2: I like that of the original thing.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:51.920
Speaker 2: And if we are not careful, we would make it seem like we are doing the same thing.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:52.280
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:53.660
Speaker 1: Affirming what they are doing.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:05:00.540
Speaker 1: And it can be an illusion to us too, that because you see somebody do this, you think like if he did it too.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:15.260
Speaker 1: Let's have a look at some of what this seeming parallels between the New age spirituality movement and Christianity is, number one, is prophetic pictures and vision boards.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:19.740
Speaker 1: You know, it's a powerful thing in our faith that we see that God took Abraham.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:20.280
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:21.920
Speaker 1: And showed him a prophetic picture.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.440
Speaker 1: Look at the stars.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.640
Speaker 1: And as many as you can count, so shall your descendants be.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.680
Speaker 1: Now this is something that we see in our faith, a prophetic imagery.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:32.240
Speaker 2: Yes.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:40.360
Speaker 1: But the difference is that in our faith, God is the one who gives the vision and it's not the other way around.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:40.800
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:45.200
Speaker 1: But we see in the New Age spirituality movement it's about what do you want?

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:45.760
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:53.490
Speaker 1: If you create, if you look at it long enough, if you can conjure it long enough, it will become yours.

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:56.970
Speaker 1: So it's still self centered, it's still self driven.

00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:00.210
Speaker 1: You know, you claim that the universe can give you a new car.

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:06.250
Speaker 1: The universe Will give you a career, will give you a car, I mean a wife, give you a job and all of that.

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:07.490
Speaker 1: It's all self driven.

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:12.620
Speaker 1: But in Genesis 15, it was God who took Abraham out.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:14.220
Speaker 2: So powerful.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:15.180
Speaker 2: So powerful.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:21.900
Speaker 2: Because at the end of the day you would see something powerful.

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:25.300
Speaker 2: But now it has been perverse.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:25.740
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:30.540
Speaker 2: That at the end of the day prophetic pictures are powerful.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:33.420
Speaker 2: And that is the thing with the devil.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:40.980
Speaker 2: The devil, even when he spoke to Adam and Eve, he took the statement of God and twisted it a little bit.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:45.060
Speaker 2: God said, you will die.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:50.580
Speaker 2: He said, well, you will not surely die.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:57.380
Speaker 2: And then he brought in the idea of the fact that you will become God self.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:03.300
Speaker 2: So taking what God did, and it's the same thing he does over and over again.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:07.190
Speaker 2: And so I think it's something to really watch out for.

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:13.150
Speaker 2: Because there is a way that you can talk about it that it seems like we are saying the same thing.

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:16.270
Speaker 2: But ours is God driven, theirs is self driven.

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:19.070
Speaker 1: In fact, primarily all through.

00:07:20.189 --> 00:07:23.230
Speaker 1: The second one is confessions and affirmations.

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:29.870
Speaker 1: You know, one thing that is peculiar to our faith is that we have a God who spoke things that were not as though they were.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:35.140
Speaker 1: And it's our practice too in our faith to speak know the things that God has said about us.

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:39.860
Speaker 1: We can, in fact, when we are declaring scriptures, we are literally speaking, we a chosen generation.

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:41.940
Speaker 1: We are a royal priesthood and all of that.

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:46.100
Speaker 1: In New Age, we see a lot of confessions.

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:47.380
Speaker 1: Things like, I am abundant.

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:49.539
Speaker 1: Yeah, money comes to me easily.

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:50.820
Speaker 1: I attract good things.

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:55.300
Speaker 1: And from the surface you see somebody doing that, you're like, that's a man of faith.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:00.710
Speaker 1: But what is driving it?

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:07.150
Speaker 1: You know, I like something you said earlier, while we're still speaking, before we started this discussion.

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:11.070
Speaker 1: And you said that the difference is that we say what God has said about us.

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:17.790
Speaker 1: That's our confession and our affirmation, not what we think or what is propelled by self.

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:20.510
Speaker 1: Yeah, that's not how we have our conversations.

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:25.880
Speaker 2: All right, so the next thing we want to talk about is meditation.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:39.400
Speaker 2: Now, if you study the New Age practices, there is something that they do whereby they sit still in a private place and then they are trying to meditate.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:44.280
Speaker 2: And many times they are trying to enter into an altered spiritual consciousness.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:50.200
Speaker 2: And some of these people actually leave their bodies during practices like this.

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:59.180
Speaker 2: Now it is possible that when Christians talk about meditation and when they talk about meditation, just because it's the same words does not mean we are doing the same thing.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:14.460
Speaker 2: Because there's a way the Bible introduces meditation for example, in Joshua, chapter 1, verse 8, it says, this book of the law shall not depart from your mouth, but you shall meditate therein day and night, that you may observe to do according to word that is written therein.

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:16.090
Speaker 2: So in Christian meditation, we.

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:28.040
Speaker 2: What we are trying to do is to actually retain the consciousness of what the word of God said, not trying to get into an altered spiritual state or anything like that.

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:34.880
Speaker 2: In fact, there is a use of the word meditation, I think, in the Hebrew that actually means to mutter.

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:35.360
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:48.050
Speaker 2: So when you are declaring the words under your breath, basically informing your mind what the word of God says, now that's far different from what the New Agers do.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:56.410
Speaker 2: And finally, to one that is very controversial, manifestation versus faith.

00:09:56.410 --> 00:10:00.490
Speaker 1: You know, if you're not careful, you see, you know, the creation and wisdom for the manifestation.

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:17.880
Speaker 2: And it is quite interesting because I've seen many Christians use the term that I'm manifesting something into existence, not knowing that the word has a source and it has nothing to do with our faith.

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:28.520
Speaker 2: And because if you're not Christian, if you're not careful as a Christian, you will find yourself dabbling into things just because they look like what the Bible says.

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:39.880
Speaker 2: But it's actually not because faith is not trying to conjure something from the universe or in our own context, conjure something from God.

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:47.440
Speaker 2: Rather, faith is an alignment to what God had already said.

00:10:47.440 --> 00:11:01.640
Speaker 2: So when God showed Abraham the stars and said, look, your descendants will be as innumerable as the stars in the heavens, then the Bible says Abraham believed God and he accounted it to him for righteousness.

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:02.680
Speaker 2: That is faith.

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:07.800
Speaker 2: Faith is believing God, not trying to conjure something for yourself.

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:17.440
Speaker 1: This is something that we must clearly, clearly separate from the beliefs of the new Ages.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:19.760
Speaker 1: Okay, now to the core of everything.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:34.810
Speaker 1: We had this entire discourse not just to speak or, you know, have a level of criticism and all, but to give a holistic viewpoint and a baseline so that when you get to interact with anybody who believes any of this, you know how to really speak.

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:42.570
Speaker 1: And also, it's important for you to know what their beliefs are so you can defend even your own beliefs and your faith as a believer.

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:48.570
Speaker 1: But now, at the core, how do you preach to people with New Age beliefs?

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:55.450
Speaker 1: You know, let's do the same thing, the same approach that Paul used in Acts, chapter 17.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:59.290
Speaker 1: First of all, he acknowledged that they were deeply religious.

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:00.250
Speaker 1: They are religious.

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:06.710
Speaker 1: But after that, he began to force them to engage their minds, to begin to think and ask them questions.

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:10.390
Speaker 1: He says, if our writers Say like you said earlier, we are his offsprings.

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:15.590
Speaker 1: He says, if we are flesh and bones, how is he made of silver?

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:21.510
Speaker 1: He says, for in him we live, move and have our being that was powerful just to engage their minds.

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:26.740
Speaker 1: So one of the first things is to start with questions and not criticism.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:29.860
Speaker 1: This is probably the most important principle.

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:33.260
Speaker 1: If you start attacking their beliefs, you will lose them too early.

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:40.460
Speaker 1: And I've seen from my experience that there's a quick defense from these people because they already feel like you have a bias.

00:12:40.940 --> 00:12:45.980
Speaker 1: Because, okay, because I'm not a Christian, because I'm not this, because I'm not that they will quickly throw at you.

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:46.820
Speaker 1: What about Muslims?

00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:47.620
Speaker 1: What about Buddhists?

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:48.380
Speaker 1: What about that?

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:49.580
Speaker 2: Does God hate them?

00:12:49.580 --> 00:12:50.620
Speaker 1: Does God hate them?

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:52.220
Speaker 1: Those are the quick questions.

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:59.780
Speaker 1: So you want to be careful and start with basic questions like, okay, how did you arrive at your beliefs?

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:07.300
Speaker 1: Ask questions like, do you believe there is such a thing as objective spiritual truths from their viewpoint?

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:11.060
Speaker 1: And you know, I've mentioned this book twice.

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:22.070
Speaker 1: I think mere Christianity and one of the things I like that book for is that it hits the core because we are all made by God and God has put something instinctively in us.

00:13:22.390 --> 00:13:27.510
Speaker 1: If the right questions are asked, the right answers will emanate regardless of what you believe.

00:13:27.910 --> 00:13:30.870
Speaker 1: And that's the power of just objective questions.

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:33.710
Speaker 1: Once you ask, is there objective spiritual truth?

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:39.990
Speaker 1: The answer will make you find the loophole through which you can give answer that we engage their minds even further.

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:43.670
Speaker 1: Where do you think spiritual truth comes from?

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:47.510
Speaker 1: How do you determine whether a spiritual experience is genuinely good?

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:50.930
Speaker 1: Can a spiritual experience ever be misleading?

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:54.530
Speaker 1: Ask them, okay, what happens after death?

00:13:55.890 --> 00:14:00.370
Speaker 1: Your objective is not to win an argument, it's to understand their worldview.

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:11.170
Speaker 1: Just like Paul understood the worldview of those in Areopagos in Athens, and he could use from the fabric of what they believe to influence their perspective of God.

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:14.130
Speaker 2: I think that is very powerful.

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:21.240
Speaker 2: And I think the next thing is now establish the question of authority.

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:26.360
Speaker 2: How do you know what you believe is true?

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:41.400
Speaker 2: Because one of the things you will find with New Agers is the fact that majority of their responses will come from I felt was real to me and it's basically experience.

00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:46.590
Speaker 2: But now what makes the interpretation of that experience true?

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:47.830
Speaker 1: How do you validate it?

00:14:47.830 --> 00:14:48.910
Speaker 2: How do you validate it?

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:55.550
Speaker 2: Because you see, one of the things that I found quite interesting is the fact that every faith is excused.
